course: dictionary
lesson: 11676
title: Assumptions: beliefs without proof
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An assumption is something you believe is true without proof. «Ella made an assumption that the shop was open, because its lights were on.» Ella had no proof, only a guess based on the lights.
explain: Scientists and mathematicians make assumptions on purpose to solve problems. «Assume the car travels at a steady speed» means we accept that speed as true, then work out the answer from there.
explain: In everyday life, a wrong assumption can be unfair. «Sam assumed Priya could not play football just because she wore glasses.» Judging people by how they look is making a wrong assumption.
explain: Useful phrases to remember are make an assumption, based on the assumption that, and a wrong assumption. Before you accept an assumption, ask yourself: is there any proof?
fact: An assumption is something you believe is true without proof.
fact: «Assume» is the verb, and «assumption» is the noun that goes with it.
fact: «Based on the assumption that» means you are starting from something you accept as true.
fact: In a formal sense, «assumption» can mean taking on a job, power or responsibility, as in «the assumption of power».
fact: The Assumption is a Christian feast day for Mary, the mother of Jesus.
exercise: «Ella made an assumption that the shop was open.» What is an assumption? | *Something you believe is true without proof | A loud noise at night | A fact you have checked with a map | A kind of petrol station
exercise: Which phrase means you are starting from something you accept as true? | A wrong answer | *Based on the assumption that | Once upon a time | Make a fine
exercise: «The team worked on the assumption that the bridge was safe.» Find and copy the word that names something believed without proof. | *assumption | safe | bridge | worked
test: «Sam's assumption that Priya could not play, just because she wore glasses, was unfair.» Why was it unfair? | It was too long | *He judged her without knowing what she could do | Priya was not in the team | It was about the weather
test: In science, why might we make an assumption? | To make a list of colours | *To start solving a problem by accepting something as true | To prove a story is a fairy tale | To ignore the problem completely
exam: What does «a wrong assumption» mean? | To be very quiet | To give a prize for the best answer | To check facts very carefully | *To believe something that is not true, without proof
